# Line-ending policy.
#
# This matters more than it looks. Git for Windows defaults to
# core.autocrlf=true, so without this file an edit committed from Windows can
# land in the repository with CRLF line endings. A shell script committed that
# way fails on every Linux and macOS checkout with
#
#     /usr/bin/env: 'bash\r': No such file or directory
#
# which is a confusing failure for anyone who did not introduce it. Pinning the
# policy here makes it independent of each contributor's local git config.

# Normalise all text to LF inside the repository
* text=auto

# ...and keep LF in the working tree too, where it is load-bearing
*.sh    text eol=lf
*.py    text eol=lf

# Mark the executable entry points, for hosts that honour it
skills/*/tests/*.sh linguist-language=Shell

# Never touch these if one is ever committed deliberately
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.gif   binary
*.pdf   binary
*.gz    binary
*.zip   binary
*.bam   binary
*.cram  binary
*.h5ad  binary
*.loom  binary
*.pyc   binary
